{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790713518,"nonce":"g+UugyFW3WQ7LKavMPDhG5H4qno=","title":"Box class symbols with alias types as reflective references","message":"In unit.TestReflect.TYPES, List and Date are class values, but their expression types appear as a TypeAlias and an unresolved TypeId. Array\u003cDynamic\u003e lowering used the expression type to choose a box and called haxe_box_int_ptr on their stable class IDs. Type.getClassName then treated the box's heap payload address as a type ID and returned null. The failure was reproducible as FAILVALUES null != haxe.ds.List, followed by null != Date.\n\nThe MIR after specialization and every optimization pass still contains the correct List class ID 282993166. The bad representation is in the static array initializer: an Int box around that ID. This checkpoint uses the class symbol's stable ID and a reference box when the expression's static type is an alias or unresolved. TestReflect advances past List and Date and now fails at Xml, whose class name also fails in a direct Xml token probe because Xml is absent from the runtime registry.\n\nValidation: haxe-conformance/regressions/TypeAliasClassTokens.hx and the no-cache application preset; focused TestReflect run; authored regression suite and full tiered suite.","files":null}]}